{"gid":"2026_01_BAL_IND","week":1,"away":"BAL","home":"IND","away_col":"#9E86FF","home_col":"#4F9BE8","spread":-3.5,"total":49.5,"fav":"BAL","hpts":23,"apts":26,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 13 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Nan","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Zay Flowers</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 IND concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58), and BAL funnel 26% of targets his way. A rec-yards spot.</p><p>The <strong>IND</strong> welcome the <strong>BAL</strong> as underdogs of <strong>3.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>49.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>BAL 26\u201323</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>33% of its snaps with BAL in front</strong>, <strong>42% within one score</strong>, and <strong>25% with BAL chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>A <strong>modest edge to BAL</strong>. They should lead more often than not, tilting them slightly toward the run late, while IND leans a touch more pass-heavy to stay in it \u2014 but the effect is gentle; this isn't a script that dramatically reshapes anyone's usage.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Jonathan Taylor</strong> \u2014 BAL are stingy against the run (94 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Lamar Jackson</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 IND allow <strong>253 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Daniel Jones</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 BAL allow <strong>257 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Mark Andrews</strong> has room to work \u2014 IND allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Alec Pierce</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 BAL concede <strong>74 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58). A rec-yards spot.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is a bell-cow (76% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.47 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to BAL \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Derrick Henry</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>BAL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>BAL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>30%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 4-point favorite projected to lead for 33% of snaps, <strong>BAL</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>16 carries for 76 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as BAL salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Zay Flowers</strong> (6 targets, 57 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when BAL lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Mark Andrews</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 29 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>IND \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>IND are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 IND project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Jonathan Taylor</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 76 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Alec Pierce</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, IND funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>5 targets for 50 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tyler Warren</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 46 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"BAL","col":"#9E86FF","players":[{"name":"Lamar Jackson","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":200.9,"book":219.5,"over_th":139.12,"under_th":218.69,"over_am":"\u2212114","under_am":"\u22